WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
If you have shipped UI kit or Swift UI apps, you carry a strong mental model of threading.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.600
React Native's architecture is built on these same primitives,

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.600
meaning your existing knowledge of the iOS Run Loop is the key to diagnosing performance problems.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.400
This chart shows how familiar Swift concepts map to the React Native engine.

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.400
Where you rely on dispatchq.main and Swift, React Native uses the UI threat.

6
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.600
Where you use a background dispatchq, React Native uses the JS thread.

7
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.000
A running React Native application maintains three primary threads.

8
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.600
The main thread is the exact same UI run loop you know from iOS.

9
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:47.200
All UI view mutations, gesture recognizer callbacks, and core animation updates land here.

10
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:53.200
The JS thread is a dedicated backgroundq managed by Hermes, the built-in JavaScript engine.

11
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.200
This is where your component render functions and business logic execute.

12
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.600
Think of the JS thread as a user interactive grand central dispatchq

13
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.400
that you cannot interact with directly from Swift.

14
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.600
The foundational contract of iOS development remains unchanged across this boundary.

15
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.800
Block any of these primary threads and you drop frames or crash the process.

16
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.000
Fabric React Native's modern rendering system manages the third architectural pillar,

17
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.800
the shadow thread. This is a dedicated C++q designed specifically to compute yoga flex box layouts

18
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.200
every time your component tree changes.

19
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.800
In UI kit terms, this operates identically to running a background GCD task

20
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:38.600
that calculates CRECT frames for an entire view hierarchy before touching the screen.

21
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.600
Fabric computes these layout calculations on the shadow thread

22
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.400
and delivers them to the main thread in a single atomic transaction.

23
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.800
By offloading layout computation to a background C++ thread,

24
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.600
the main thread remains entirely unburdened by heavy view measurements.

25
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.400
Historically, React Native suffered from a persistent one-frame lag.

26
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.200
Gestures felt slightly disconnected and fast scrolling could expose blank cells before the layout caught up.

27
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.600
This timeline shows the cause, the legacy bridge architecture.

28
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:13.000
Data passing between the JS and main threads relied on asynchronous JSON serialization,

29
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.400
guaranteeing that layout calculations were always at least one frame behind the event cycle.

30
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:24.000
The modern architecture replaces that bridge with the JavaScript interface or JSI.

31
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.200
JSI allows synchronous C++ calls between the JS thread and the shadow thread,

32
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.400
eliminating the serialization step.

33
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.200
Layout calculations now happen synchronously

34
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.800
and the resulting view mutations commit directly to the main thread.

35
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.400
For an iOS developer, this is the exact same latency reduction you experience

36
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.000
when migrating from cross-thread notification center broadcasts to direct method calls.

37
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.200
Eliminating the asynchronous JSON bridge is the specific technical shift

38
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.600
that allows React Native animations to hit 120 frames per second.

39
00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.600
Transitioning from Swift to React Native requires a shift in how you handle thread safety.

40
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.000
We call this the concurrency inversion.

41
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.200
This diagram illustrates the Swift paradigm.

42
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:12.600
Swift concurrency uses at main actor to explicitly pull background work into the UI thread.

43
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.200
In React Native, the flow is reversed.

44
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.800
JavaScript is single threaded by design.

45
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.400
All components state, reducers, and render paths execute inherently on the JS thread.

46
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.800
The challenge runs in the opposite direction.

47
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:31.000
You must actively push heavy computation off the JS thread and on to native worker threads

48
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.200
to protect your execution budget.

49
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.000
When you need to update a view property from the background,

50
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.200
you map Swift's dispatchq.main.async to react native reanimated run on UI worklet directive.

51
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.000
For standard animations, setting use native driver to true,

52
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
operates exactly like CA basic animation.

53
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.400
It moves interpolation entirely to the native realm by passing the JS thread completely.

54
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.600
Swift's compiler will warn you if you misuse main actor.

55
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.600
React Native provides no such compile time safety.

56
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.800
Calling an expensive synchronous operation like sorting a massive array

57
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.600
or parsing a large JSON string silently stalls the JS thread.

58
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.400
Mastering concurrency here means treating the JS thread as a strict bottleneck

59
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.000
that requires constant offloading rather than a destination for your tasks.

60
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.800
Let's look at memory management.

61
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.600
Swift uses automatic reference counting or ARC.

62
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.000
The Hermes engine uses a generational garbage collector.

63
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.600
ARC distributes memory overhead deterministically across retain and release calls.

64
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.800
Hermes, however, relies on a compact phase that stops the world.

65
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.000
When JavaScript holds on to unbounded object graphs,

66
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.800
like massive redux doors or uncashed image descriptors,

67
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.800
the heap swells and the compact phase takes longer to execute.

68
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.600
A prolonged garbage collection compact phase pauses the JS thread entirely.

69
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.200
When the JS thread completely stops, the layout passes skip.

70
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.600
If a screen exhibits intermittent visual jank without any obvious CPU load,

71
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.800
a Hermes garbage collection pause is almost certainly the culprit.

72
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.800
Modern iOS hardware introduces a strict timing constraint.

73
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.200
At 60 frames per second, you have 16 milliseconds to render a frame.

74
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:23.000
On a 120 hertz promotion display, that budget shrinks to exactly 8 milliseconds.

75
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.200
A 10 millisecond task on the JS thread goes completely unnoticed on an older device.

76
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:32.400
However, on a modern iPhone 15 Pro, it guarantees a dropped frame

77
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.000
because the strict deadline arrives before the work completes.

78
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.800
This representation of the Xcode time profiler helps diagnose overflows.

79
00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.000
Isolate the thread labeled calm.facebook.react.javascript.

80
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.200
A problematic trace reveals a massive vertical spike,

81
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.200
breaching the 8 millisecond deadline during idle time.

82
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.000
To prevent frame drops during complex transitions,

83
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.400
UI kit developers defer work with async after.

84
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:02.200
The react made of equivalent is interaction manager.runafter interactions.

85
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:07.200
By queuing non-critical data fetches until after navigation transitions

86
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.600
clear the active interaction registry,

87
00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.000
you protect that tight 8 millisecond frame budget.

88
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:19.000
The underlying operating system enforces the exact same rules for UI kit and react meev.

89
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Success relies entirely on keeping the threads unclogged

90
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.000
and measuring the results in instruments.

